    "$permissive_means": "PERMISSIVE policies are ORed together, RESTRICTIVE ones are ANDed with the result. That is why the eight deny_all_client_roles policies are RESTRICTIVE and why the count alone would have missed the point: a PERMISSIVE policy saying `false` adds nothing and is defeated the moment somebody adds a second permissive policy, while a RESTRICTIVE one cannot be outvoted by any policy added later. Moving these tables from zero policies to RESTRICTIVE false is therefore a strengthening, not a restatement: zero policies is also undefeatable, but only until the first permissive policy lands. profiles_super_admin_mfa is the ninth RESTRICTIVE policy and is not a deny-all: it ANDs an aal2 requirement onto every UPDATE of a super_admin profile."
  },
  "client_dml_grants": {
    "$what": [
      "Which client roles hold INSERT, UPDATE or DELETE, measured against production on 2026-09-10.",
      "A policy decides which ROWS a role may write; a grant decides whether it may write at all.",
      "This file already recorded the policy side (write_policies). Nothing recorded the grant side,",
      "and docs/SECURITY-POSTURE.md section 4 calls that the one structural gap in the schema."
    ],
    "$measured_at": "2026-09-10",
    "$how": [
      "The query is in docs/SECURITY-POSTURE.md section 4 and in migration 230's header.",
      "src/lib/auth/rls-manifest.test.ts is the CI gate over this block and needs no database."
    ],
    "$why_it_grows": "Supabase's default privileges hand the client roles everything on every new table, so each migration that creates one adds three more write privileges unless it says otherwise. 56 relations at the 2026-09-01 reading, 74 on 2026-09-10. This block is what makes the next increase visible instead of silent.",
    "totals_2026_09_10": {
